<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- Finder's Action Extension and the app exchange an admission receipt
	     plus completed staged copies here. This Mac-style group starts with
	     the Developer Team ID so directly distributed Developer-ID builds can
	     access it without a provisioning-profile prompt. No source path is
	     ever written into the group: admission is granted before anything
	     reads source bytes. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>88M28542LQ.com.hausfold.perch</string>
	</array>
	<key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- Watched folders: the grant a folder picker hands over dies with the
	     process, and watching ~/Downloads across relaunches means keeping it.
	     This lets perch persist each picked folder as an app-scoped security
	     bookmark — still only folders the user chose in a panel, one bookmark
	     each, revocable in Settings. A watched folder copies what
	     arrives and never touches the original. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.files.bookmarks.app-scope</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- The update check: one hourly GET to api.github.com for the latest
	     release tag, off by a Settings toggle — see
	     Perch/Platform/UpdateCheck.swift. Perch never uploads anything.
	     It fetches exactly one kind of file, and only when someone clicks
	     Update on a drag install: the release ZIP, into this container
	     (Perch/Platform/SelfUpdate.swift). The install itself happens
	     outside this sandbox, in PerchUpdater.app — nothing perch does with
	     this entitlement writes outside the container. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- The mobile shelf listener: paired iPhones deliver to the shelf over
	     the local network (Bonjour _perch._tcp, end-to-end encrypted — see
	     Perch/Mobile/). Nothing else ever listens. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.network.server</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- haus's drop: ~/.config/perch/{config.json,themes/*.json}, which
	     lives outside the container. Read-only, one directory, and the only
	     path perch reaches for that a drag or a file picker did not hand it.
	     Three readers, all of that one directory: Perch/UI/Theme/RicePalette.swift
	     (the palette), Perch/UI/Settings/ScreenshotsFolder.swift (where this
	     Mac saves screenshots, which is only ever used to aim a file picker —
	     reading it grants nothing), and Perch/App/AppConfigFile.swift, where a
	     key this file names declares the matching Settings switch.

	     READ-ONLY, and it stays that way. Settings is file-backed, but the file
	     it WRITES is ~/Library/Containers/…/Application Support/Perch/settings.json
	     — perch's own container, which needs no exception at all. Widening this
	     one to read-write so both could be the same file would trade a narrow,
	     explicable exception for one that lets perch write anywhere under a
	     home-relative path, which is the kind App Review reads as a sandbox
	     that isn't one. Layering the two files costs less than that. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.temporary-exception.files.home-relative-path.read-only</key>
	<array>
		<string>/.config/perch/</string>
	</array>
	<!-- The Dock's own preference domain, read-only, one domain: perch reads
	     `enterMissionControlByTopWindowDrag` to know whether macOS's top-edge
	     Mission Control trigger is armed, because while it is, it eats every drag
	     aimed at the notch and perch looks broken by its only gesture. Measured
	     2026-08-26 on macOS 26.6: without this key EVERY route to that value —
	     `UserDefaults(suiteName:)`, `CFPreferencesCopyAppValue`, and reading
	     ~/Library/Preferences/com.apple.dock.plist — answers nil/false, so perch
	     could not tell an armed Mac from a fixed one. With it, the two preference
	     reads answer and the FILE read stays denied: this widens the sandbox by
	     exactly one domain's preferences, and nothing on disk.

	     Read-only, and it stays that way. Perch never writes this key — flipping
	     someone's system setting from an app is not perch's to do, so the nudge
	     opens System Settings ▸ Desktop & Dock and lets them decide. See
	     Perch/Platform/MissionControlCheck.swift.

	     `com.apple.screencapture` answers the other question perch cannot
	     otherwise ask: where this Mac saves screenshots (`location`, absent on a
	     stock Mac, which means the Desktop). Measured the same way, 2026-08-31 on
	     macOS 26.6, with two signed one-line probes in real app bundles: sandboxed
	     without this domain the read is nil, sandboxed with it the real path comes
	     back. It is read for exactly one purpose
	     — aiming the folder picker in Settings ▸ Watched Folders at the folder the
	     person is looking for — so reading it grants nothing and a wrong answer
	     would only cost a navigation. Guessing instead was worse than it looks:
	     the offered folder is also how Settings decided a folder was "the
	     screenshots one", so a wrong guess quietly attached itself to a folder
	     someone added for another reason. See
	     Perch/UI/Settings/ScreenshotsFolder.swift.

	     Perch never writes this key either. Where screenshots go is macOS's
	     setting and the person's choice; perch only wants to stop asking them
	     to type it. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.temporary-exception.shared-preference.read-only</key>
	<array>
		<string>com.apple.dock</string>
		<string>com.apple.screencapture</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
